  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Why Containers Need Their Own Network & Storage",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Process, Not A VM",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Container = host process wrapped in namespaces + cgroups.",
            "Isolation comes from kernel primitives, not virtual hardware.",
            "Two costs: ports unreachable outside, filesystem dies with it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Namespace",
            "Gives the container"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "net",
              "own NICs, routes, iptables"
            ],
            [
              "mnt",
              "own mount tree"
            ],
            [
              "pid / uts",
              "own PID 1, own hostname"
            ],
            [
              "ipc / user / cgroup",
              "own SHM, UIDs, limits"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Writable Layer",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Image layers read-only + one thin writable layer on top.",
            "Copy-on-write: touching 1 byte copies the whole file up.",
            "Slow when write-heavy, unshareable, tied to container life."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run --rm alpine true  # RW layer dropped",
            "docker ps -a --size          # SIZE = RW layer",
            "docker system df             # what stopped ones hold"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where Each Thing Lives"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Thing",
            "Home"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "App code",
              "the image"
            ],
            [
              "Config",
              "injected at run time"
            ],
            [
              "State",
              "a volume"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Disposable Rule",
          "text": "12-factor: if `docker rm` loses something you care about, it should have been a volume."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Proof And Route Ahead"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run -it alpine ip addr",
            "# 1: lo   2: eth0@if12",
            "# no host wlan0, no docker0 = net ns"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "namespaces",
              "bridge",
              "ports",
              "DNS",
              "drivers",
              "volumes",
              "permissions",
              "debug"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Netns And The veth Pair",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A netns holds interfaces, IPs, routes, ARP, netfilter rules.",
            "veth = virtual cable: in one end, out the other.",
            "Container end is `eth0`; host end `vethXXXX` joins `docker0`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "tcpdump -i docker0 port 5432",
            "tcpdump -i vethb2f1        # one container",
            "# see exactly what crosses the bridge"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "ip link | grep veth   # vethb2f1@if11 (host)",
            "docker exec c ip link # eth0@if12",
            "# if-numbers cross-reference the two ends"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Why Containers Need Their Own Network & Storage (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "yaml",
          "lines": [
            "- type: bind",
            "  source: ./src",
            "  target: /app",
            "  read_only: true       # long form wins"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Inspect What Got Wired",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker network inspect bridge   # IPs + MACs",
            "F='{{.NetworkSettings.IPAddress}}'",
            "docker inspect -f $F c          # 172.17.0.3"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "localhost Is The Container",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every container gets its own `lo` at 127.0.0.1.",
            "So `localhost` inside means the container, never the host.",
            "The single biggest source of beginner confusion."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "P=$(docker inspect -f '{{.State.Pid}}' c)",
            "nsenter -t $P -n ip addr",
            "# works on images with no shell tools"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Traps To Know Early"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "veth/bridge MTU defaults 1500; VPN hosts hang on big payloads.",
            "Small pings pass, large transfers stall — MTU hides well.",
            "Container IPs are reassigned on restart. Never hardcode them."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run --network=container:web tools",
            "# same IP, same ports, one namespace",
            "# this is how k8s pods and sidecars work"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Default vs User-Defined",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Trait",
            "default bridge",
            "user-defined"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "DNS by name",
              "no",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "Isolation",
              "all can talk",
              "per-network"
            ],
            [
              "Live attach",
              "no",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "Subnet control",
              "daemon-wide",
              "per-network"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`docker0` is usually 172.17.0.0/16 — joined when no `--network`.",
            "Only the deprecated `--link` gave names on the default bridge.",
            "Compose builds one per project, named `<project>_default`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Attach, Detach, Multi-Home"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker network create mynet",
            "docker network connect mynet c   # live",
            "docker network disconnect bridge c"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One container can sit on many networks at once.",
            "It gets `eth0`, `eth1`, … one IP per network.",
            "Standard shape: proxy fronting an isolated backend."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Reading The Wiring & The Bridge Driver",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Subnets And Sealed Tiers"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker network create --subnet 10.20.0.0/16 \\",
            "  --gateway 10.20.0.1 mynet",
            "docker run --net mynet --ip 10.20.0.9 db",
            "docker network create --internal dbnet"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "--internal",
          "text": "No outbound NAT at all — the database tier physically cannot reach the internet."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "ICC And Pool Limits",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Daemon `--icc=false` blocks default-bridge container chatter.",
            "Prefer user-defined networks over fiddling with ICC.",
            "`no available, non-overlapping IPv4 address pool` = too many nets."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker network prune          # reclaim ranges",
            "# or daemon.json:",
            "# \"default-address-pools\": [ ... ]"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Publishing, Host Side First",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Flag",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "-p 8080:80",
              "host 8080 to container 80"
            ],
            [
              "EXPOSE 80",
              "documents only, publishes nothing"
            ],
            [
              "-P",
              "every EXPOSEd port to random high port"
            ],
            [
              "docker port c",
              "shows what -P actually chose"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Order Matters",
          "text": "Reversing `-p CONTAINER:HOST` is the classic bug — the host port always comes first."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Bind, Protocol, Ranges"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "-p 127.0.0.1:8080:80   # loopback only",
            "-p 8080:80             # binds 0.0.0.0",
            "-p 53:53/udp           # TCP is default",
            "-p 8000-8010:8000-8010 # a whole block"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Happens Underneath",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Docker writes a DNAT rule in the nat table `DOCKER` chain.",
            "Destination is rewritten to the container IP.",
            "A MASQUERADE rule covers outbound traffic."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "iptables -t nat -L DOCKER -n",
            "# DNAT tcp dpt:8080 to:172.17.0.3:80"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`docker-proxy` is a userland fallback for cases iptables misses.",
            "Example: host-local loopback hairpin traffic.",
            "It is why a process appears to hold the port."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Publishing Ports: -p, EXPOSE, and NAT",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Three Ways It Bites"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Docker's rules sit ahead of most UFW/firewalld rules.",
            "So `-p 8080:80` punches through a firewall you thought closed.",
            "Bind to 127.0.0.1, or set `iptables: false` and own the rules.",
            "Outbound needs no publishing — NAT works by default."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "# bind: address already in use",
            "ss -ltnp | grep 8080   # who has it"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Host Mode",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No net namespace — the host stack and port space directly.",
            "Fastest: no NAT, no veth hop. Zero isolation, real collisions.",
            "`-p` is ignored with a warning; there is nothing to map."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Desktop Catch",
          "text": "On macOS/Windows the 'host' is the Linux VM, not your laptop — host mode does not expose your machine's network."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "None And LAN-Native",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`--network none`: a namespace holding only `lo`.",
            "Use for batch jobs that must have no connectivity.",
            "macvlan: own MAC + IP on the physical LAN, no NAT.",
            "macvlan needs promiscuous mode; host-to-container is blocked."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker network create -d macvlan \\",
            "  --subnet 192.168.1.0/24 \\",
            "  -o parent=eth0 lan",
            "# ipvlan: same, shares host MAC (L2/L3)"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Overlay Across Hosts"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "VXLAN-based multi-host networking for Swarm services.",
            "Encapsulates container traffic between nodes.",
            "`--attachable` lets standalone containers join too."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Driver Cheat Table",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Driver",
            "NAT",
            "Isolation",
            "Needs -p"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "bridge",
              "yes",
              "yes + DNS",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "host",
              "no",
              "none",
              "no (ignored)"
            ],
            [
              "none",
              "n/a",
              "total",
              "n/a"
            ],
            [
              "macvlan",
              "no",
              "LAN-native IP",
              "no"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Host, None, macvlan, ipvlan, overlay",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reaching Back To The Host"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "# Desktop: host.docker.internal just works",
            "docker run --add-host=\\",
            "  host.docker.internal:host-gateway app",
            "# or use the bridge gateway IP on Linux"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`docker network ls` shows driver and scope (local vs swarm).",
            "`bridge`, `host`, `none` are built in and cannot be removed.",
            "Default to a user-defined bridge; host only when measured."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Embedded DNS Begins"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Resolver at 127.0.0.11 in every user-defined-network container.",
            "Names served: container name, `--hostname`, `--network-alias`.",
            "Misses forward to the daemon's upstream resolvers."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker exec c cat /etc/resolv.conf",
            "# nameserver 127.0.0.11",
            "# upstream from --dns or daemon.json"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Aliases And Scaling",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Many containers on one alias = many A records.",
            "Crude round-robin, but clients cache DNS — not a balancer.",
            "`docker compose up --scale web=3` returns all three IPs."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "DNS is per-network: two networks, names from both.",
            "On the default bridge a container resolves no peers at all.",
            "musl (Alpine) queries nameservers in parallel — flaky lookups."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Debug A Name",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker exec c getent hosts other",
            "docker exec c nslookup other",
            "docker exec c cat /etc/resolv.conf"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "--link Is Legacy",
          "text": "It injected /etc/hosts entries and env vars, one-directional, and broke across restarts. Use user-defined networks."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Pattern To Copy"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "App dials `postgres:5432` — a service name, not an IP.",
            "No ports published, so the DB is unreachable from the host.",
            "Restarts change IPs; the name keeps resolving."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Service Discovery, Then Mount Types",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Three Mount Types",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "Lives",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "named volume",
              "/var/lib/docker/volumes",
              "production state"
            ],
            [
              "bind mount",
              "any host path",
              "dev source, config"
            ],
            [
              "tmpfs",
              "RAM only",
              "secrets, scratch"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "-v myvol:/data                 # named",
            "-v /srv/app:/app               # bind (abs!)",
            "--tmpfs /tmp                   # RAM",
            "--mount type=tmpfs,dst=/tmp,tmpfs-size=64m"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "-v Versus --mount"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A relative bind path is parsed as a volume NAME, not a path.",
            "`-v` auto-creates missing host paths, root-owned — a surprise.",
            "`--mount` is explicit key=value and errors on a missing source.",
            "Prefer `--mount` in scripts, `-v` at the prompt."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Volume Seeding Rule"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Empty named volume on a populated path: image content copies in.",
            "Bind mounts never seed — they shadow whatever was there.",
            "The copy happens once, only while the volume is empty.",
            "Stale volume keeps old files: 'my code changes don't appear'."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Hardening A Mount",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run --read-only \\",
            "  -v conf:/etc/app:ro \\",
            "  --tmpfs /run app   # writable scratch"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`-v /data` with no name makes a random-hash anonymous volume.",
            "Dockerfile `VOLUME /data` does that on every single run.",
            "They pile up silently until you prune."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Picking Between Them",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Axis",
            "Volume",
            "Bind"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Portability",
              "wins",
              "host-tied"
            ],
            [
              "Live editing",
              "no",
              "wins"
            ],
            [
              "Linux speed",
              "equal",
              "equal"
            ],
            [
              "Desktop speed",
              "fast",
              "much slower"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Decision Line",
          "text": "Bind mount for dev source and host config; named volume for databases and production; tmpfs for secrets and scratch."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The CLI Surface"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker volume create|ls|inspect|rm|prune",
            "docker volume inspect myvol   # Mountpoint",
            "# /var/lib/docker/volumes/myvol/_data"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Service Discovery, Then Mount Types (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Volumes outlive containers by design; `docker rm c` keeps them.",
            "`docker rm -v c` drops that container's ANONYMOUS volumes only.",
            "`volume prune` deletes anything no container references.",
            "Compose prefixes names with the project; `down -v` deletes them."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker volume ls -f dangling=true  # check first",
            "docker volume prune                # then destroy"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sharing And Inheriting",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One volume, many containers is normal — app writes, sidecar backs up.",
            "No cross-container locking; coordinate writers in the app.",
            "`--volumes-from other` inherits all mounts (old data-container trick)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Drivers And Labels"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker volume create --driver local \\",
            "  --opt type=nfs --opt device=:/exp/data \\",
            "  --opt o=addr=10.0.0.5,rw nfsvol",
            "docker volume ls -f label=project=app"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Backup Idiom"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker run --rm -v myvol:/data \\",
            "  -v $(pwd):/backup alpine \\",
            "  tar czf /backup/vol.tgz -C /data .",
            "# restore: untar into a fresh volume"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Don't `cp` into /var/lib/docker/volumes as a workflow.",
            "It bypasses bookkeeping and breaks on non-local drivers.",
            "DB data dirs (`/var/lib/mysql`) = named volume, never Desktop bind.",
            "Shared filesystems break fsync and locking semantics."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Numbers, Not Names",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Mounts carry numeric UID/GID; the kernel only checks numbers.",
            "The container's /etc/passwd names mean nothing to the host.",
            "UID 1000 inside is UID 1000 outside — `appuser` there, you here."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker exec c ls -lan /data",
            "# drwxr-xr-x 2 1000 1000 ...  numeric!",
            "# Permission denied = UID mismatch"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Three Ways To Fix It"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Fix",
            "Where"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "--user $(id -u):$(id -g)",
              "run time"
            ],
            [
              "adduser -u 1000 + USER",
              "Dockerfile"
            ],
            [
              "chown -R 1000:1000 ./data",
              "host dir"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "docker",
          "lines": [
            "RUN adduser -u 1000 -D app",
            "COPY --chown=app:app . /srv",
            "USER app"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Named Volumes: Lifecycle, Drivers, Sharing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`--user` with an unknown UID: `whoami` fails, `$HOME` unset.",
            "It cannot be changed after the container starts.",
            "It breaks images that drop privileges themselves.",
            "Official Postgres starts as root then `su-exec` to postgres."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Volumes Are Gentler",
          "text": "First use copies the image's content AND its ownership into the empty volume — a correctly-owned image dir just works. Bind mounts get no help."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Root Is Root",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "By default container root IS host root on a bind-mounted file.",
            "Only `userns-remap` in daemon.json breaks that equivalence.",
            "This is a genuine privilege-escalation path, not a theory."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Never Mount The Socket",
          "text": "Bind-mounting /var/run/docker.sock into an untrusted container is equivalent to handing it root on the host."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Platform Traps"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "-v ./data:/data:Z   # SELinux private",
            "-v ./data:/data:z   # SELinux shared",
            "# without it: Permission denied on RHEL"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Desktop bind mounts pass a translation layer, reporting 0777.",
            "So permission bugs first surface on the Linux deploy.",
            "Test ownership on Linux before you trust it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Compose Networking",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One default bridge per project; every service joins it.",
            "Services resolve each other by service name. No `links` needed.",
            "Extra networks: top-level `networks:` plus a per-service list."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Ports And Volumes Keys",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "yaml",
          "lines": [
            "ports:",
            "  - \"8080:80\"      # quote it, always",
            "expose:",
            "  - \"80\"           # documents only"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unquoted `5432:5432`-style values get parsed oddly by YAML.",
            "Top-level `volumes:` declares the named volumes.",
            "`external: true` means it exists — don't create or delete it."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Compose: Networks and Volumes Declared",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Same Slot, Two Meanings",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "yaml",
          "lines": [
            "volumes:",
            "  - ./src:/app          # bind mount",
            "  - appdata:/var/lib/data  # named volume"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Waiting Properly"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`depends_on` waits for start, never for readiness.",
            "Pair it with a `healthcheck` and `condition: service_healthy`.",
            "Otherwise the app dials a database that isn't listening yet."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Patterns Worth Stealing",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Base file mounts nothing; override adds source bind + hot reload.",
            "Production runs the base file alone.",
            "`network_mode: host` kills Compose networking and voids `ports:`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "yaml",
          "lines": [
            "volumes:",
            "  - ./app:/app",
            "  - /app/node_modules   # anon vol shields it"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Command",
            "Removes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "down",
              "containers + network"
            ],
            [
              "down -v",
              "also named volumes"
            ],
            [
              "down --remove-orphans",
              "deleted services"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Debug Step One"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "docker inspect c   # read .NetworkSettings",
            "#                  # and .Mounts",
            "docker port c      # published, for real",
            "ss -ltnp           # host is listening?"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Meant vs Wired",
          "text": "`inspect` shows what actually got wired. Your compose file only shows what you meant to wire."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Refused Versus Timeout",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Symptom",
            "Likely cause"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Refused from host",
              "app bound to 127.0.0.1"
            ],
            [
              "Refused between containers",
              "different networks"
            ],
            [
              "Refused, right net",
              "used host port, not real one"
            ],
            [
              "Timeout",
              "firewall, routing, MTU"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Your container can't be reached and your database just vanished — same root cause: namespaces gave it a private network and a disposable filesystem. This handbook walks the whole path: veth pairs and bridges, what `-p` really writes into iptables, embedded DNS at 127.0.0.11, volumes vs bind mounts vs tmpfs, and the numeric UID traps that only break after you deploy. Ten pages of study notes — save it before the next Permission denied.",
